The period from 1711 to 1733 covers the last twenty-two years of the [[Sinchew Colony]], from its entry into the [[First War of the Isthmus]] to the establishment of an independent state at the end of the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]]. It is known in Sinchewese historiography as the '''Revolutionary Period'''.

The colony entered the period as a possession of [[Zong]] with a population of about three and a quarter million, and left it as a republic. In the intervening years it fought a war in the isthmus at the direction of the crown, was refused compensation for the enslaved labour it had contributed, was required to emancipate the remainder at its own cost, watched the mainland receive elected representation in the [[General Assembly]] while its own seats remained the crown's to fill, and produced the three bodies whose union carried the revolution.

Two governors held office in the period: [[Tân Khéng-chong]] from 1703 to 1719 and [[Lîm Bûn-khiat]] from 1719 to 1733. The second was the last governor of the colony.

== First War of the Isthmus (1711–1716) ==

{{Main|First War of the Isthmus}}

The colony entered the period at war. The [[First War of the Isthmus]] was fought from April 1711 to January 1716 between a Zong-led coalition of the commonwealth constituents and a [[Volisania|Volisanian]]-led coalition, over the control of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the reclamation of [[Free Sinchew]].

The war was begun by the colony's own naval officers. Admiral Lim Kiat-seng, a visiting officer of the Royal Zong Navy stationed at Tamsui, exaggerated an incident concerning three Zong trading vessels, imposed a communications blackout on Governor Tân Khéng-chong, and despatched a flotilla against Sant Cristòfor without authorisation. The attack of 18 April 1711 was under way before the court at Zong learned of it, and imperial approval was granted after the fact.

The colony raised troops for the land war on the Sinchew–Balboa border, which was the most destructive theatre of the war, and equipped large regiments of enslaved labourers for it under the slave-backed loan system established in October 1711. About 5,000 enslaved Sinchewese were deployed and about 3,000 were killed. The war ended in January 1716 with the [[Treaty of Sant Cristòfor]] and the reunification of Free Sinchew into the colony.

== Aftermath of the isthmus war (1716–1718) ==

The colony had raised large regiments of enslaved labourers for the land war of 1711 to 1716 under the slave-backed loan system established in October 1711, in which the colonial government issued loans secured against the value of enslaved people contributed by landowners. Of the approximately 5,000 enslaved Sinchewese deployed, an estimated 3,000 were killed.

The Zong court refused to compensate the landowners who had contributed them, holding that enslaved people had not legally existed under the [[Zong Edict of 1500]] and that loans secured against their value were therefore void. The refusal extinguished the investments of the colony's landowning class, which had lent its labour to the war in expectation of return or payment.

A rebellion of the landowners followed in 1717 and was suppressed. The colonial administration put it down with the garrison, and the political repression that followed it removed the landowning class from public life at the moment its capital had been destroyed.

The emancipation edict of 1718 required the colony to legislate the abolition of slavery within its territory and to free the remaining enslaved population without compensation to their owners. The plantation economy depended on enslaved labour, and its collapse followed within the year. The Zong court provided no financial assistance, regarding the emancipation as a measure the colony was obliged to carry out at its own cost.

The economic crash and the refusal of assistance produced resentment that ran beyond the landowning class. The colony had fought a war at the direction of the crown, lost a third of the labour it had sent, been denied payment for it, and then been required to free the remainder at its own expense.

== Constitutional grievance (1718–1722) ==

The colony's position within the kingdom became the subject of organised complaint in these years, and the terms of the complaint were constitutional rather than economic.

{{Main|General Assembly}}

The [[General Assembly]] had been created by the [[Merchants' Revolution]] of 1643. The precedent for non-royal representation in the Zong legislature had been established by the concessions to Louyang and Sinchew after the Yuanlong Restoration, when the colonial administrations received seats in the [[Societal Assembly]] — held by royal appointees rather than by representatives the colonies chose. The Merchants' Revolution extended that precedent to the mainland, granting the localities of Zong the right to elect their representatives.

The colonies were left with their appointed seats. Sinchew and Louyang were represented in the General Assembly by men appointed by the crown, in a body that had just given the right of election to the towns of the mainland. The colony was therefore governed by a legislature it could not elect, and the measure that had enfranchised the mainland had passed without altering its position.

The complaint took the form of a demand for elected representation rather than of a demand for independence, and it was made by a population that had no reason to prefer the crown to the colony's own administration.

=== Liberal thought ===

The argument was supplied from three directions.

The General Assembly's own example. The Merchants' Revolution had demonstrated that a Zong legislature could be elected, that merchants and localities could be represented, and that the change could be made without the kingdom's dissolution. The colony's reformers argued from the settlement their own kingdom had made.

Liberal writing from the mainland. The constitutional literature produced in Zong after 1643 circulated in the colony, and it treated representation, consent and the limits of executive authority as matters of general application rather than of mainland privilege.

Illypnian political writing. Works from [[Illypnia]] reached the colony through the trade that had connected it to the Illypnian world since the 1560s, and they supplied a vocabulary of natural rights, of government by consent and of the legitimacy of resistance that the Zong literature had not developed. The colony's position at the meeting point of the two spheres gave its readers access to both.

== Formation of the movements (1722–1726) ==

Three bodies were founded on three different constituencies, and the revolution was made by their union.

=== Sinchew Liberal Assembly ===

The [[Sinchew Liberal Assembly]] was founded by the colony's landowning class, which was Tianist and which had been ruined by the emancipation and the voiding of the slave-backed loans. It sought elected representation in the General Assembly and the reform of the colony's government on the mainland model, and its members were the men the emancipation had dispossessed.

=== Union of Free Peoples ===

The [[Union of Free Peoples]] was founded by the Baiduist population of the colony and by the Hahkina of the former [[Hachinawa]], whose land had passed to Zong settlers through legal settlement in the preceding period and whose migration out of the province had been prohibited in 1679.

It continued the second object of the [[Brotherhood of the Free Peoples]], which had held that the Hahkina, the Baiduists and the other peoples subject to the colony had a common position and that a polity of their own would have to include them all. It sought the independence of the colony rather than representation within the kingdom.

=== Second Ocean Crossing Society ===

The Second Ocean Crossing Society was founded by the Baiduist population and named for the [[Ocean Crossing Society]] that had raised the [[Baiduist revolutionary army]] in 1648. It organised among the Baiduist towns and settlements, and claimed the inheritance of the society that had been suppressed in 1656.

== Approach to the revolution ==

By 1726 the three bodies had reached a common position. The Liberal Assembly sought representation and the reform of the colony's government; the Union of Free Peoples sought independence; the Second Ocean Crossing Society sought the restoration of the movement of 1648. Their objects were not identical and were not reconciled in the period.

The [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] began in 1726. The revolution succeeded where the [[Sinchew War]] of 1648 had failed, and the difference is the coalition: the earlier uprising had been raised by the Baiduist population alone, and the revolution was made by the Baiduists, the Hahkina and the Tianist landowning class together.

== Sinchewese Revolutionary War (1726–1733) ==

{{Main|Sinchewese Revolutionary War}}

The revolution began in 1726 and ended in 1733 with the establishment of an independent state.

It succeeded where the [[Sinchew War]] of 1648 had failed, and the difference is the coalition that made it. The uprising of 1648 had been raised by the Baiduist population of the colony alone, and it was defeated at Tamsui by the colonial garrison and the imperial fleets. The revolution was made by three bodies with three different objects — the [[Sinchew Liberal Assembly]], the [[Union of Free Peoples]] and the [[Second Ocean Crossing Society]] — whose union gave it the countryside of the former [[Hachinawa]], the Baiduist towns and the landowning class at the same time.

The war lasted seven years and the colony's population fell in the course of it: the return of 1730 records 3,417,899 against 3,480,965 in 1725, the first decline recorded in the colony's returns since they began in 1540.

== Demographics ==

The administration continued to take a return every five years until the war. The classes recorded are those of the period, and one of them changes name in the interval: the enslaved class disappears from the returns of 1720, and the emancipated population appears in them as a class of its own.

