The classes are those of the colonial returns, and they name the colony's peoples by three different conventions at once. Tianist and Baiduist name religions, and the two classes are the colony's own population divided by confession. Hahkina is the annexed kingdom's own name for its people, kept because the colony had governed them as a state. Mahik is the Hahkina's word for the unkinged peoples of the region, which the Zong administration had used since the 1530s and retained in its returns. Sitonglan is the emancipated population's name for itself, in Zong.

The enslaved population fell from 118,829 to 103,447 between 1710 and 1715, the losses of the isthmus campaign being concentrated in that class, and it is absent from the returns of 1720 and afterwards. The emancipations had freed the population without compensation to their owners, and it appears in the returns from 1720 as Sitonglan.

The two classes are the same people under two names. The population recorded as 103,447 enslaved in 1715 is recorded as 109,240 Sitonglan in 1720, the difference being births over the interval net of the losses of the war. The emancipations of 1718 altered the status of the population and did not alter the population.

The Sitonglan (失鄉人, from ''sit-hiong-lâng'', "people who have lost their home") were the Dagiti, Balausian and Marosan peoples carried to the colony by the [[Secret Trade]] and its successors. They came from three peoples and became one in the colony, and the name they took was Hokkien: the language they had in common was the one they had been brought to, and the name described what had happened to them rather than what they were. They had been brought from the north of [[Fosia]] and could not return to it, and they were a population with no homeland to appeal to.

The colony's population reached its highest recorded figure in 1725, at 3,596,283, and the return of 1730 is the first in the series to record fewer people than its predecessor. The war and the dislocation of the revolution account for the difference.

The Tianist population rose to 210,043 by 1725 and fell to 206,118 by 1730. It was the class from which the [[Sinchew Liberal Assembly]] was drawn.

The Baiduist and Hahkina populations, which between them comprised about ninety per cent of the colony, rose throughout the period to 1725 and fell in the return of 1730.

